#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101683} :activity/id #uuid "ba6b04cd-0e14-5cbc-8d10-bae9558ae0bb" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049008} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101683 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101961 :tutorial/content "Remember that the **identity matrix** $I_n$ is an $n \\times n$ square matrix with $1$'s on the diagonal, and where all other elements are $0.$ For example, the $3 \\times 3$ identity matrix $I_3$ is given below:\n\n$$\nI_3 = \n\t\t\t\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe identity matrix gets its name from the fact that whenever we multiply a matrix by the corresponding identity matrix, the matrix remains unchanged.\n\nFor example, let's consider the matrix\n\n<div class=\"matrix-operands matrix-operands-stacked\">\n<div>$A=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 3 & 10 \\\\ 6 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 23 & 4 & 8\\end{bmatrix}$</div>\n</div>\n\nMultiplying the matrix $A$ by the identity matrix $I_3,$ we find that the result is just $A$ itself:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A\\cdot {I}_{3} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 3 & 10 \\\\ 6 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 23 & 4 & 8\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}(-2)\\cdot 1+3\\cdot 0+10\\cdot 0 & (-2)\\cdot 0+3\\cdot 1+10\\cdot 0 & (-2)\\cdot 0+3\\cdot 0+10\\cdot 1 \\\\ 6\\cdot 1+(-2)\\cdot 0+2\\cdot 0 & 6\\cdot 0+(-2)\\cdot 1+2\\cdot 0 & 6\\cdot 0+(-2)\\cdot 0+2\\cdot 1 \\\\ 23\\cdot 1+4\\cdot 0+8\\cdot 0 & 23\\cdot 0+4\\cdot 1+8\\cdot 0 & 23\\cdot 0+4\\cdot 0+8\\cdot 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 3 & 10 \\\\ 6 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 23 & 4 & 8\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =A\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $A \\cdot I_3 = A.$ You can check that $I_3 \\cdot A = A$ using the same method.\n\nThis result holds in general: \n\n> For *any* square $n \\times n$ matrix $A,$ we have\n> \n> \n> $$\n> A \\cdot I_n = I_n \\cdot A = A.\n> $$\n\nIn other words, the $n \\times n$ identity matrix $I_n$ has a similar property to the number $1,$ namely that \n\n$$\na\\cdot 1 = 1\\cdot a = a\n$$\n\n for any real number $a.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "5383efb1-6e51-562a-bf79-9ab8cba87ed7" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2995 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "880e64ac-57e9-5d82-9ecc-43590af3ef76" :step/math-academy-id 16190 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101684}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101684 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100875 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1f2f5234-df9c-5269-8702-30b4dde9a2ca" :knowledge-point/title "Multiplying a Matrix by the Identity Matrix"} :step/id #uuid "c7473014-2f99-59a1-b6cb-a5a8ad7926aa" :step/math-academy-id 16191 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101685}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101685 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101962 :tutorial/content "Whenever we multiply a matrix $A$ by the $n$th column of the identity matrix, the result is the $n$th column of $A.$\n\nFor example, consider the matrix\n\n<div class=\"matrix-operands matrix-operands-stacked\">\n<div>$A=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$</div>\n</div>\n\nThe first column of the identity matrix $I_2$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nIf we multiply $A$ by the column on the *right*, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}3\\cdot 1+(-1)\\cdot 0 \\\\ 1\\cdot 1+2\\cdot 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe product is just the first column of the matrix $A.$\n\nUsing the same method, you can check for yourself that when we multiply $A$ by the second column of $I_2,$ the result is the second column of $A.$\n\nOn the other hand, if we multiply $A$ by the first *row* of the identity matrix $I_2$ on the *left*, we will obtain the first row of $A$. Indeed,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}3\\cdot 1+1\\cdot 0 & (-1)\\cdot 1+2\\cdot 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "2c46b879-fe8a-5f8e-8579-71b1bc92ac07" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2996 :tutorial/title "Multiplying a Matrix by a Column of the Identity Matrix"} :step/id #uuid "3cad7c8a-e8e1-5ac0-bdff-f4886c348fb7" :step/math-academy-id 16193 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101686}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101686 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100876 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "89362140-1a17-56fd-a9ab-4da336112bee" :knowledge-point/title "Multiplying a Matrix by a Column or Row of the Identity Matrix"} :step/id #uuid "2a8c2469-c4d7-571d-a243-2cf1fe09ed32" :step/math-academy-id 16194 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101699}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101699 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101965 :tutorial/content "We've been using the fact that $AI_n = I_n A = A$ for any $n \\times n$ matrix $A.$ But how do we know for sure?\n\nLet's prove the case of $n=2.$ That is, let's prove that $AI_2=I_2A = A,$ where $A$ is any $2\\times2$ matrix.\n\nAny general $2 \\times 2$ matrix can be written as\n$$\nA =\n\t\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$ \n\nThen, multiplying by $I_2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A{I}_{2} & =\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{11}\\cdot 1+{a}_{12}\\cdot 0 & {a}_{11}\\cdot 0+{a}_{12}\\cdot 1 \\\\ {a}_{21}\\cdot 1+{a}_{22}\\cdot 0 & {a}_{21}\\cdot 0+{a}_{22}\\cdot 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{2}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe above proof shows that the property $A I_2 = I_2 A = A$ holds for all $2\\times2$ matrices $A.$\n\nIn general, to prove that the property $AI_n = I_n A = A$ holds for all $n \\times n$ matrices $A,$ we can use the same method." :tutorial/id #uuid "4c2408f2-4c4d-5bc6-bd0c-e649f5e542f1" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3132 :tutorial/title "Proving Properties Related to the Identity Matrix"} :step/id #uuid "acbf3f49-a158-5e75-b433-69f7dd172d90" :step/math-academy-id 17078}] :activity/title "Multiplying a Matrix by the Identity Matrix" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186101038}]}
